(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["mth-12-13"] = {"6.1":{"s":"A differential equation is an equation that contains the derivative of an unknown function. Many laws of physics, chemistry and other sciences are written this way, so we need to find the functions that satisfy them. Examples are dy/dx = cos x and d²y/dx² + k²y = 0."},"6.2":{"s":"The order of a differential equation is the order of the highest derivative in it. The degree is the power of that highest derivative once the equation is written as a polynomial in its derivatives, with no derivative left under a root or in a fraction. Order and degree are always positive whole numbers.","w":["Clear roots and fractions of derivatives first, then read off the degree; it may not exist for equations like sin(dy/dx) = x."]},"6.3":{"s":"To form the differential equation of a family of curves, write the general equation with its arbitrary constants, differentiate as many times as there are constants, and eliminate the constants between these equations. The result has the same order as the number of arbitrary constants.","w":["Differentiate once for each arbitrary constant; the order of the equation equals that number."]},"6.4":{"s":"A solution of a differential equation is a function that satisfies it. The general solution holds the arbitrary constants and describes all solutions; a particular solution is got by giving those constants definite values. A differential equation therefore has infinitely many solutions, and we verify a solution by differentiating and substituting.","w":["The number of arbitrary constants in the general solution equals the order of the equation."]},"6.5":{"s":"Variable separable: if the equation can be written as f(x) dx = g(y) dy, integrate both sides. Some equations become separable after a substitution such as x + y = u. Homogeneous equations use y = vx. A linear equation dy/dx + Py = Q is solved by multiplying by the integrating factor e raised to the integral of P dx.","f":[{"x":"f(x) dx = g(y) dy","n":"Variable separable form: integrate both sides.","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 2, p.198"},{"x":"I.F. = e^(∫P dx)","n":"Integrating factor of dy/dx + P*y = Q (P, Q functions of x).","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 2, p.204"},{"x":"y*(I.F.) = ∫Q*(I.F.) dx + c","n":"General solution of the linear equation dy/dx + P*y = Q.","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 2, p.204"},{"x":"x*(I.F.) = ∫Q*(I.F.) dy + c","n":"For dx/dy + P*x = Q (P, Q functions of y), with I.F. = e^(∫P dy).","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 2, p.204"}],"w":["Put the equation in the standard form dy/dx + Py = Q (coefficient of dy/dx equal to 1) before finding P."]},"6.6":{"s":"Differential equations model real changes. If a quantity changes at a rate proportional to itself, the equation is of the form dm/dt = km, as in radioactive decay or population growth. We form the equation from the statement, solve it, then use given values to find the constants. The slope of a curve is dy/dx, so conditions on slopes also give differential equations.","w":["For decay the constant k is negative; for growth it is positive."]}};
